Define a function $f \colon \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1} \to \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}$ by
\begin{equation*}
    f(n) = \sum_{i = 1}^n \sum_{j = 1}^n j^{3272} \left\lfloor\frac1j + \frac{n-i}{n}\right\rfloor.
\end{equation*}
Let $M=2 \cdot 3 \cdot 5 \cdot 7 \cdot 11 \cdot 13$. Assume that $M < 1000$. Let $N = f{\left(M^{7}\right)} - f{\left(M^{7}-1\right)}$. Let $k$ be the largest non-negative integer such that $2^k$ divides $N$. What is the remainder when $2^k$ is divided by $20^3$?

Let $f \colon \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1} \to \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}$ be a function such that for all positive integers $m$ and $n$, 
\begin{equation*}
    f(m) + f(n) = f(m + n + mn).
\end{equation*}
The sum $\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{617} f(n)$ is required to be $500\,000$.
Across all functions $f$ such that $f(n) \leq 617$ for all $n \leq 617$, how many different values can $f(3520)$ take?

